### How do you make a synchronous GET request?

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import java.net.URI;
import java.net.http.*;
import java.time.Duration;

public class HttpClientDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
            .connectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
            .build();

        HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
            .uri(URI.create("https://api.bank.example/accounts/SAV2001"))
            .header("Authorization", "Bearer token123")
            .GET()
            .build();

        // Synchronous: blocks until response
        HttpResponse<String> response = client.send(
            request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());

        System.out.println("Status: " + response.statusCode());
        System.out.println("Body: " + response.body());
    }
}
```

### How do you make an asynchronous request?

```java
// Asynchronous: returns CompletableFuture immediately
client.sendAsync(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString())
    .thenApply(HttpResponse::body)
    .thenAccept(body -> System.out.println("Async body: " + body))
    .join();  // wait for completion in demo
```

### How do you make a POST request?

```java
String json = "{\"holder\":\"Rajesh\",\"amount\":50000}";

HttpRequest postRequest = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
    .uri(URI.create("https://api.bank.example/transfers"))
    .header("Content-Type", "application/json")
    .POST(HttpRequest.BodyPublishers.ofString(json))
    .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
    .build();

HttpResponse<String> postResponse = client.send(
    postRequest, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());
```

### How is HttpClient better than HttpURLConnection?

| Feature | HttpURLConnection | HttpClient (Java 11) |
|---------|------------------|---------------------|
| API style | Verbose, manual streams | Clean builder pattern |
| HTTP/2 | Not supported | Built-in |
| Async | Not supported | sendAsync + CompletableFuture |
| Connection pooling | Manual | Automatic |
| Thread safety | Not reusable | Immutable, thread-safe |
| Body handling | Manual InputStream | Typed BodyHandlers |

### What are common mistakes?

WRONG: Creating a new HttpClient per request.

```java
// WRONG: wasteful -- creates new client each time
HttpClient bad = HttpClient.newHttpClient();
HttpResponse<String> resp = bad.send(req, BodyHandlers.ofString());
```

CORRECT: Reuse a single client instance.

```java
// CORRECT: create once, reuse everywhere
private static final HttpClient CLIENT = HttpClient.newBuilder()
    .connectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
    .build();
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- HttpClient uses a shared executor (ForkJoinPool.commonPool) for async operations by default. For production, provide a custom executor via `HttpClient.newBuilder().executor(myExecutor)`.
- For Spring applications, `WebClient` or `RestClient` are typically used instead of raw HttpClient, but HttpClient is the standard library option with zero dependencies.
- HttpClient supports WebSocket via `HttpClient.newWebSocketBuilder()`.
- The client handles redirects, authentication, and cookie management via builder configuration.
